 ],
 "literal": "Whoever you may be, taking the rear guard and the vanguard / avoid saying 'we will fight', [he of] lofty strength / the chief of the warriors with long spears that shine and glitter / with a majestic chest wearing fine ornaments that emit rays / before seeing my lord of excellent shoulders!",
 "translation": "Whoever you may be, whether you lead the rear guard or the vanguard, avoid saying, 'We will fight!' before you have laid eyes upon my lord of excellent shoulders, the chief of the spear-wielding warriors of lofty strength, whose majestic chest is adorned with fine, radiant jewels.",
 "modern": "நீங்கள் யாராக இருந்தாலும், உங்கள் பின்னணிப் படையையும் முன்னணிப் படையையும் கொண்டு, 'நாங்கள் போரிடுவோம்' என்று சொல்வதைத் தவிர்ங்கள். ஒளிர்கின்ற நீண்ட வேலை ஏந்திய மழவர் குலத் தலைவனும், கதிர்களை வீசும் நுட்பமான அணிகலன்களை அணிந்த அழகிய மார்பையும் சிறந்த தோள்களையும் உடையவனுமாகிய என் தலைவனைக் காண்பதற்கு முன்னே!",
 "explanation": "The poet Avvaiyār is addressing the rival kings who are preparing to wage war against her patron, Atiyamāṉ Neṭumāṉ Añci. She warns them to refrain from boasting about their military strength, whether they lead the vanguard or the rear guard. She advises them that declaring 'we will fight' is a grave mistake before they have even seen her lord. Atiyamāṉ is described as the formidable chief of the Malavar warriors, possessing lofty strength and wielding shining spears. The sight of his majestic chest, adorned with radiant jewels, and his powerful shoulders would strike fear into any challenger, making them realize the futility of their boast.",
